I've seen the question, "How long does it take to get your first ascension?"
posed on RGRN more than once. Well, for me it was approximately 17 months
and 14 days. I first downloaded the game on 19 Dec 2002, and have been
addicted since.

I've played all different classes, races, alignments... I seemed to have
most of my success playing Wiz-Hum-Mal-Neu (usually named Abe), up to one
specific game late last year. Everything was clicking; early magic lamp
(SDSM +3), early Magicbane, breezed through the Mines, Sokoban and the Quest
(a first!). Made it to the castle, acquired the WOW. By far and away, my
best game ever. Then I chickened out. I didn't want to go any farther and
risk a YASD, so I took a peek into the Valley, then saved the game. I
figured I'd gain some more experience, learn some more, then go back and
finish.

I took a hiatus from the game during the holidays, then early February or so
starting playing again. But, instead of playing another in the long, long,
long line of male wizard characters named Abe, I switched up and played my
first female character, still a wizard but gnomish, named Eba. To the most
part, these went as most of the other characters in my past- relatively
early YASDs or such. I generally don't add a number to my characters' names
unless they reach a certain milestone, be it an XP level or dungeon level.
This Eba 4 was probably around the 10th incarnation of her persona.

This game started relatively unremarkably. Original spellbooks were FB and
Detect Monsters. Rings were Slow Digestion (always useful) and +1
Protection. Wand was something forgettable. No magic marker or blindfold. As
the game started, I didn't take any notes, because I didn't sense anything
special about this character. In hindsight, the one thing I do remember was
aggressively trying to lower my AC by any means possible (probably brought
on by having the ring of Protection). I always had known the importance of a
low AC, but never truly appreciated it or pursued it in the early game. I
had always looked to getting better weapons or spellbooks, not armor. Now,
after all this time, maybe I know better! Before I had a ?oIdentify, I
remember checking B/U/C of different armor at altars, then trying them on to
check their enchantment. I think by the time I reached DL 14, I had a
negative AC. Eventually, after gathering enough gold, I had a naked AC of 0,
thanks to an aligned altar in Minetown.

Another aspect of the game that helped my success was an early spellbook of
Charm Monster. Whenever I got into any trouble, this spell helped get me
out. At times, I had a small army. This especially helped in the Sokoban
zoos to take the heat off of me a bit (Sokoban provided a much needed BoH).
Thanks to a polymorph trap somewhere in the dungeon, I eventually had a pet
Disenchanter (the first one I had ever seen!) that stayed with me until we
became separated in Gehennom.

The turning point in this game was converting altars on levels just before
and after Medusa. I had picked up a spellbook of Create Monster along the
way, so I engaged in a sacrificing frenzy. Finally picked up Magicbane, then
Vorpal Blade, Werebane, Frost Brand (the one I wanted), and lastly
Trollsbane. (Ended up getting Demonbane from an Archon in Vlad's Tower as
well.) While praying between sacrifices I received many gifts- this was the
first time I've prayed on an altar for anything other than to acquire holy
water, and boy did it pay off! I now see the benefits- why I never did this
before, I don't know. I received the castle passtune, protection (about 3
times), Speed, Stealth, many spellbooks, and was crowned (all of which were
obviously firsts for me).

With Magicbane and Frost Brand in hand, I continued on to the castle (Medusa
had gone down without a whimper, thanks to a found amulet of reflection). My
army easily captured the castle and the WoW inside, which resulted in an
amulet of life saving, +2 SDSM, +2 Speed Boots, +2 Helm of Brilliance, a
magic marker, and a +oPolymorph (I had 2 wishes remaining at endgame). With
all of the spoils left behind from the vanquished monsters in the castle and
at the nearby altars, I alchemised and polypiled like mad. Polypiling
resulted in gauntlets of dexterity and a t-shirt (at last!). With the
scrolls and potions I accrued, I was able to enchant my armor up (my AC
always stayed in the mid/high -40s afterward), my key weapons up to +6, and
add HP and Pw all the way up into the 300s. I knew at this point I had a
real good chance to make it.

With all of this and more than 14 XP levels under my belt, I went back up to
take on the Quest. Having only one completed Quest in my past (which was a
long, arduous battle), I expected the worst, but in fact it was a piece of
cake. The Dark One hardly put up a fight. Bell acquired, I went back down to
Fort Ludios. Never having been there before, I didn't know what to expect,
but it was again strikingly easy. I don't remember even seeing Croesus- I
think my army (now bolstered by dragons and the like) took him out before I
could even get to him. I gathered up all the loot, but not long afterward
dumped all of the gold due to the weight.

Down to the castle I went again. Here I stood again- the same point where I
had previously lost my nerve. Eba seemed much stronger and more complete
then my last candidate, so I jumped in. The Valley of the Dead wasn't much
of a challenge at all. "This is it? This will be easy!" I thought to myself.
Yeah, right.

I went down a few levels, still pretty easy. But then I hit Asmodeus' level.
My first major demon. "I'll take this cautiously," I said. I slowly started
trudging through the maze, pets in tow. I got a bit ahead of my pets, and
the next thing I knew, bang! Right through a trap door, into Juiblex's
swamp. Fortunately, I didn't drown (and had had the foresight to grease my
BoH), but within 3 turns I was engulfed and sick. Needless to say, I was
panicked. I stopped, collected my composure, then drew from my RGRN
experience: I remembered something about a /oDigging. I gave it a try, and
sure enough, it worked! I was out, but still sick. I killed Juiblex with one
blow, then fished a !o full healing out of my BoH. Healed and safe- Whew!
"Let's go back up and gather my thoughts." I never saw what hit me.

Next thing I know, I'm surrounded by nasties, getting hit by Asmodeus, then
Dispater shows up, then Yeenoghu. On top of this, there's an arch-lich
summoning more monsters. At this point, I saved the game, and pleaded to
RGRN for advice (thanks to all who responded). A few days later (and much
calmer), I jump back into the fray. I called my army (which had been left
behind after my fall into the swamp) to me with my magic whistle to provide
a cushion between myself and my attackers. Not long afterwards, I noticed an
enemy cockatrice fall in battle, so I forced my way over to it. Wielding the
rubber chicken, I quickly took out the 3 demon lords, and also Orcus (who
had been summoned by the arch-lich). I then saved the game again, just in
case my luck would run out.

Upon re-starting the game, I quickly stoned the arch-lich, then went back up
to the castle (and my stash) to lick my wounds and relearn my spells. A
little more polypiling and potion scrounging put my HP up in the 400s and XP
level in the high teens. Even though the demon horde didn't really put much
of a dent into my HP, I knew it was only going to get worse so I wanted to
be prepared. At this time I genocided L, ; and h for good measure. As it
turned out, Asmodeus' Lair was the storm before the calm. Baalzebub was
defeated easily, Orcus was already a pile of rocks, and Vlad didn't put up
much of a fight either. I sneaked (snuck?) past Rodney's tower (where
gremlins were quickly multiplying), down down down to the bottom.

Most of the game after this point is a blur. I had found and marked the
Vibrating Square, had 3 (later 4) amulets of life saving, and had in hand
the Bell and the Candelabrum. I was VERY excited, to say the least. After
some self-deliberation, I decided to go for it and wake up the Wizard. I
genocided g to rid myself of the gremlin menace, then went in. All I can say
after meeting up with Rodney (17 times!) is  that I'm very grateful for
Finger of Death!

I then went down, performed the invocation (way cool!), gave the high priest
the Finger (one zap and done!), and grabbed the Amulet. I couldn't believe
it. I had it! Now what? Up!

Endgame from the depths of hell up to the Plane of Earth was relatively
uneventful, other than the "force" and visits from Rodney. I had planned
ahead, and was carrying 5 (for good measure) ?of Gold Detection, and 4 !of
Booze. The Plane of Earth was easy; remarkably so. The only downside was
when the Finger of Death directed at Rodney also took out two of my pets.
Air was much tougher- I used up quite a few wands of teleportation there. I
think Air was the hardest of the four. Fire wasn't too tough either,
especially armed with Frost Brand and Cone of Cold. Water was tough, and the
fact that the portal was covered by a pile of gold made it tougher (I
searched for it to no avail for quite some time).

Having finally stumbled onto the portal somewhat accidentally, I left all of
my pets behind. I was granted a Guardian Angel, so I wasn't completely
alone. Detect Monsters showed Famine to the left, Death in the center, and
Pestilence to the right. Figuring that neutral wasn't likely to be in the
center (the RNG couldn't be so kind!), I went right. Pestilence was waiting
for me, and quickly sickened me. At this point, I think my brain went from
"cautious, planning" mode to "panic, hack and slash" mode. I healed myself
with one of about 8 !o holy water in my possession, then went to blasting. I
had many wands with me- a few /o Death, but mostly magic missile, fire and
cold. I fired at everything in sight, taking out Pestilence twice, and
forced my way to the altar. Lawful?! Argh! Back out I went, past the newly
revived Pestilence, and fought my way towards the center (and Death).
Fortunately the Pest stayed behind, but Death proved worse, much worse. I
think he eventually drained me of 175 HP (fortunately I had 450+/- to work
with!). I managed to get past him, killing priests and angels of all
alignments as I went (no wonder Thoth was angry with me!). I got past the
attacking masses, made it to the altar... Neutral! No way! I did it! Could I
still die, 4 times...? Nope, this is real! At this point I started taking
images using Print Screen, like a Tourist with an Expensive Camera.

And here I am. Now maybe I can go back and finish that other game.

